// Concept registry for business-a (one concept ~ one lesson). Imported by build-syllabi.mjs, which PREPENDS the coverage
// label to every description ("Both boards (ZIMSEC Business Studies Form 5; Cambridge AS Level).", "Cambridge only
// (A Level only).", "ZIMSEC only (Business Enterprise Skills Form 6)."), computed from the syllabus nodes that cover the
// concept. The text below says what is IN and what is OUT; "ZIMSEC detail" / "Cambridge detail" marks content that only
// one board names. Abbreviations: ZBS = ZIMSEC Business Studies, BES = ZIMSEC Business Enterprise Skills.
// 107 concepts. Numbers in lessons are computed in code (see lib.mjs); case studies are invented, clearly fictional firms.
export const CONCEPTS = [
  // ---- Enterprise (Cambridge 1.1; BES 6.1 / 6.3; ZBS 1.1, 1.8, 3.1)
  ["abus.enterprise.business-activity", "The nature of business activity: purpose, value added and choice", "Why businesses exist and what they do (meeting needs and wants, creating employment, wealth and growth), the factors of production (land, labour, capital, enterprise) and their rewards, adding value (selling price minus the cost of bought-in inputs, and how it can be increased), scarcity, the problem of choice and opportunity cost, the dynamic business environment (change as a source of opportunity and threat), and local, national, international and multinational businesses. Sectors of the economy and ownership forms are separate concepts (abus.structure.*); why businesses fail is abus.enterprise.success-failure."],
  ["abus.enterprise.entrepreneurs", "Entrepreneurs, intrapreneurs and enterprising behaviour", "Entrepreneurs and intrapreneurs compared: qualities needed (risk-taking, creativity, determination, self-confidence, leadership, resilience), the role of entrepreneurship in creating and starting up a business and of intrapreneurship in an established one, barriers to entrepreneurship (finance, skills, regulation, market access), business risk against uncertainty, the role of enterprise in a country's development (jobs, output, innovation, tax revenue); BES detail: the meaning of enterprising, an enterprising person and an enterprise, a culture of enterprising, characteristics of a successful enterpriser, benefits and drawbacks of enterprising. Particular ventures are abus.enterprise.ventures-risks."],
  ["abus.enterprise.success-failure", "Why businesses succeed or fail, and how failure is reduced", "Causes of success (good management, adequate finance, understanding the market, innovation, quality) and of failure (poor cash management, over-expansion, weak management, competition, changes in the economy or the law, poor planning); consequences of failing for owners, workers, suppliers and the community; ways of reducing the risk of failure (planning, cash flow control, market research, training, diversification, contingency plans); Cambridge detail: failure through lack of finance - bankruptcy, liquidation and administration explained in outline. Business finance itself is abus.finance.need."],
  ["abus.enterprise.business-plans", "Business plans: purpose, content and evaluation", "The meaning and purpose of a business plan (guiding the owner, raising finance, setting targets and measuring progress), the key elements (executive summary, description of the business and its objectives, products, market analysis and marketing plan, operations and management plan, human resources, financial plan with cash flow forecast, break-even and funding required), who reads a plan and what each reader looks for, benefits and limitations, and (ZIMSEC detail) how to prepare and implement a plan step by step and evaluate each component. Project formulation is abus.project.formulation."],
  ["abus.enterprise.ventures-risks", "Enterprise ventures, opportunities, risks and constraints", "BES detail (Form 6): ways of enterprising and example ventures (bee farming, mushroom farming, vending, software and similar), finding opportunities in a local environment and judging them, risks attached to a venture and how to reduce them, constraints on enterprises (political, legal, social, ecological and economic) and why ethical behaviour matters in running a venture. The qualities of enterprisers are abus.enterprise.entrepreneurs; business ethics as a decision issue is abus.objectives.decisions-ethics."],
  ["abus.enterprise.resources-capabilities", "Resources, capabilities and acquiring resources", "BES detail: resources (tangible and intangible) and capabilities compared and why an enterprise needs both; means of acquiring resources (hire, purchase, build-own-transfer and build-own-operate-transfer arrangements); efficient and sustainable use of resources (avoiding waste, maintenance, sharing, reuse); core competences in outline. Factors of production are abus.enterprise.business-activity; lease and hire purchase as finance are abus.finance.sources-external."],

  // ---- Business structure (Cambridge 1.2; ZBS 1.1; BES 6.1, 6.2)
  ["abus.structure.sectors", "Economic sectors: primary, secondary, tertiary, quaternary, public and private", "The four sectors and examples of firms in each (extraction and farming, manufacturing and construction, services, knowledge and information activities), the chain of production, industrialisation (what it is and its effects on a country), the public and private sectors and the businesses in each (state-owned enterprises and private firms), and the reasons for and consequences of changes in the relative importance of the sectors (deindustrialisation, growth of services, technology). Ownership forms are abus.structure.unincorporated and later concepts."],
  ["abus.structure.economic-systems", "Economic systems and the state's role: privatisation, commercialisation and nationalisation", "Command, free-market and mixed economies and how businesses operate in each; the economic philosophies behind them (capitalism, socialism, communism, indigenisation); advantages and disadvantages of each system for businesses; privatisation (selling state assets to private owners), commercialisation (running a public body on commercial lines) and nationalisation (taking private assets into state ownership), the reasons for each and their advantages and disadvantages in a given situation (Cambridge A Level). Government policy tools are abus.environment.government-economy."],
  ["abus.structure.unincorporated", "Sole traders, partnerships and co-operatives", "Main features, advantages and disadvantages of the sole trader, the partnership (partnership agreement, unlimited liability, sharing profits) and the co-operative (member ownership, one member one vote, kinds of co-operative); unlimited liability and why it matters; starting a business of each type and the legal requirements in outline; the appropriateness of each form for a given business. Companies are abus.structure.companies; franchises and joint ventures are abus.structure.franchise-jv-social."],
  ["abus.structure.companies", "Private and public limited companies; limited liability", "Private limited and public limited companies: separate legal personality, limited liability and what it protects, continuity, shares and shareholders, directors, how a company is formed and the legal documents in outline, raising capital (private placement against a stock exchange listing), control and divorce of ownership from control, advantages and disadvantages, and the advantages and disadvantages of changing from one type of ownership to another (for example sole trader to private company, private to public)."],
  ["abus.structure.franchise-jv-social", "Franchises, joint ventures, social enterprises and choosing a form of ownership", "Cambridge detail: the franchise (franchisor and franchisee, fees, support, benefits and drawbacks to each), the joint venture, the social enterprise (social or environmental purpose, reinvested profit, triple bottom line link), public sector bodies in outline, and how to judge the most appropriate form of ownership for a business in a given context (liability, finance, control, risk, tax, size)."],

  // ---- Size of business (Cambridge 1.3; ZBS 1.3; BES 6.2, 6.3)
  ["abus.size.measuring", "Measuring the size of a business", "Methods of measuring size - revenue (turnover), number of employees, capital employed, profit, market share, number of outlets - the calculation of market share from sales figures, the strengths and weaknesses of each method, why one measure can mislead (capital-intensive against labour-intensive firms, different industries), and the appropriateness of each measure in a stated case. Market share and growth in marketing terms are abus.marketing.markets."],
  ["abus.size.small-business", "Small businesses and SMEs: role, strengths, problems and support", "Advantages and disadvantages of being small (flexibility, personal service, owner control against limited finance, weak buying power, dependence on the owner), strengths and weaknesses of family businesses, the importance of small firms in an economy (employment, innovation, competition, serving local needs) and their role in some industrial structures; ZIMSEC detail: the importance of SMEs in Zimbabwe, problems SMEs face (finance, markets, skills, infrastructure, regulation), solutions, state and other assistance to new enterprises and the ways the state assists or constrains businesses of different sizes."],
  ["abus.size.organic-growth", "Organic (internal) growth and managing a growing business", "Why a business grows (economies of scale, market share, security, profit, owners' ambition) and how it can grow internally (new products, new markets, more outlets, investment in capacity, franchising), the advantages and limits of organic growth, and (BES detail, Form 6) survival and growth strategies of an enterprise and strategies for managing a growing enterprise (delegation, controls, finance, staffing); problems of rapid growth and overtrading in outline. External growth is abus.size.mergers-takeovers."],
  ["abus.size.mergers-takeovers", "External growth: mergers, takeovers, joint ventures and alliances", "Horizontal, vertical (backward and forward) and conglomerate integration; friendly merger and hostile takeover; the impact of a merger or takeover on stakeholders (owners, workers, customers, suppliers, competitors); why a merger or takeover may or may not achieve its objectives (culture clash, diseconomies of scale, overpaying, integration problems); joint ventures and strategic alliances as methods of external growth. The ownership form of a joint venture is abus.structure.franchise-jv-social."],

  // ---- Objectives, ethics, stakeholders (Cambridge 1.4, 1.5; ZBS 1.4; BES 6.3)
  ["abus.objectives.aims-types", "Business aims and objectives: types, hierarchy and conflict", "Objectives of private-sector, public-sector and social-enterprise organisations (survival, profit and profit maximisation, growth, market share, social and environmental objectives, service), the importance and characteristics of objectives, the relationship between mission statement, aims, objectives, strategy and tactics (strategic, tactical and operational objectives), the triple bottom line (economic, social, environmental) and corporate social responsibility as an objective, conflicts between objectives and constraints on objectives (ZIMSEC detail), and why objectives change over time. Decision-making stages, SMART targets and ethics are abus.objectives.decisions-ethics."],
  ["abus.objectives.decisions-ethics", "Objectives in decision-making, SMART targets, constraints and ethics", "The stages of business decision-making and the role of objectives in them; translating objectives into targets and budgets; SMART objectives (specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, time-limited) and examples; communicating objectives and the likely effect on the workforce; constraints on objectives (finance, law, competition, stakeholders, resources - ZIMSEC detail); how ethics influence objectives and activities (fair dealing, honest advertising, treatment of workers, environment) and the costs and benefits of ethical behaviour, including unhu/ubuntu/vumunhu in business practice (ZIMSEC detail)."],
  ["abus.stakeholders.roles-influence", "Stakeholders: roles, influence, accountability and conflict", "Internal and external stakeholders (owners, managers, workers, customers, suppliers, lenders, government, the community, pressure groups) and their interests, rights and responsibilities; how business decisions affect stakeholders and how they react; the impact of stakeholder aims on decisions; accountability to stakeholders; how conflict arises between stakeholders with different aims (for example shareholders against workers or the community) and the effect of changing objectives; judging the relative power and influence of stakeholders in a case."],

  // ---- External environment (Cambridge 6.1; ZBS 1.2; BES 6.3)
  ["abus.environment.political-legal", "Political and legal influences on business", "How a government uses the law to control employment practices, conditions of work (including health and safety), wage levels, marketing behaviour (consumer protection, advertising), competition (monopolies and mergers), location decisions and particular goods and services; the impact of changes in political and legal factors on business decisions; for ZIMSEC the macro-environment examples (political, legal) and the laws and regulations governing start-up and operation of enterprises (registration, licences, tax, labour and consumer law) in outline. Privatisation and nationalisation are in abus.structure.economic-systems."],
  ["abus.environment.government-economy", "Government intervention, market failure and macroeconomic policy", "How a government helps businesses and encourages enterprise (grants, subsidies, tax breaks, training, infrastructure) and constrains business activity (taxes, regulation, price controls, licensing, quotas); dealing with market failure (externalities, public goods, monopoly power) in outline; macroeconomic objectives (low unemployment, low inflation, economic growth) and the effect of economic performance on business; monetary, fiscal, supply-side and exchange rate policies and the impact of changes in them on business decisions (interest rates, taxes, exchange rates). ZIMSEC state interventions to assist or constrain businesses are included."],
  ["abus.environment.social-environmental", "Social, demographic and environmental influences; CSR and sustainability", "Cambridge detail: corporate social responsibility and its issues (accounting practices, paying incentives to win contracts, social audits), why businesses must consider the community and pressure groups, demographic change locally, nationally and globally (ageing, urbanisation, migration, population growth) and its effect on business, physical environmental issues (pollution, resource depletion, climate), environmental audits, and the growing importance of sustainability for decisions. The objectives link is abus.objectives.aims-types."],
  ["abus.environment.technology-competitors", "Technological change, competitors and suppliers", "The impact of technological change on business and decisions (new products and processes, automation, communication, e-commerce, costs and jobs); the impact of competitors (market structure, competitive reactions, price and non-price competition) and of suppliers (bargaining power, reliability, cost, relationships) on business decisions. ICT resources and their role in functional areas are abus.ict.role-in-business; Porter's five forces is abus.strategy.environment-scanning."],

  // ---- International business (Cambridge 6.1.6; ZBS 1.6; BES 6.1)
  ["abus.international.trade-integration", "International trade, trade barriers, trade agreements and economic integration", "The importance of international trade and trading links to a business (markets, specialisation, supplies, competition), imports and exports, the balance of payments in outline, trade barriers (tariffs, quotas, embargoes, standards, licences, exchange controls) and how they affect firms and how firms respond, trade agreements and their effects on business, the role of technology in international trade, and economic integration - bilateral, multilateral and regional arrangements such as SADC, COMESA and ECOWAS, their benefits and drawbacks for enterprises."],
  ["abus.international.multinationals-globalisation", "Multinational companies and globalisation", "Why firms become multinational (objectives), benefits and challenges of MNCs to host and home countries (jobs, technology, tax, competition, profit repatriation, local firms, environment), relationships between MNCs and governments, problems MNCs face abroad (culture, law, currency, politics), globalisation - meaning, causes and its advantages and disadvantages for businesses and countries, and its effect on location and marketing choices in outline."],

  // ---- Strategy and decision tools (Cambridge 6.2; ZBS 3.3; BES 6.1, 6.4)
  ["abus.strategy.environment-scanning", "The business environment and tools of environmental analysis: SWOT, PEST(LE) and five forces", "Internal and external environments, macro and micro environments, and environmental scanning: the SWOT analysis (strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, threats) and how to turn it into a strategy, the PEST/PESTEL analysis (political, economic, social, technological, environmental, legal factors), Porter's five forces (rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, buyer power, supplier power) and what each force implies for profitability; applying the tools to a local business; their limitations."],
  ["abus.strategy.models", "Strategic models: blue ocean, scenario planning, core competence, force field and the Ansoff matrix", "Blue ocean strategy (creating uncontested market space against competing in a red ocean), scenario planning (thinking through possible futures and preparing responses), the core competence framework (what a firm does best that is valuable, hard to copy and extendable), force field analysis (driving against restraining forces for a change) and the Ansoff matrix (market penetration, product development, market development, diversification) with the risk of each; how a firm chooses between them and the limitations of each. ZIMSEC detail: Ansoff in product portfolio planning."],
  ["abus.strategy.decision-trees", "Decision trees and expected value", "Drawing and reading a decision tree (decision nodes, chance nodes, branches, probabilities, outcomes, costs), calculating expected values, net expected value after the cost of an option and choosing the best option, multi-stage trees, the advantages and limitations of decision trees (estimated probabilities, attitude to risk, non-financial factors); numbers are computed in code. Cost-benefit analysis is abus.project.cost-benefit."],
  ["abus.strategy.strategic-management", "Strategy, corporate planning, culture, strategic change and contingency planning", "The meaning and purpose of business strategy and of strategic management - analysis, choice and implementation; corporate planning and its importance; corporate culture (types and the effect on decision-making); transformational leadership as a driver of change (meaning and importance); managing and controlling strategic change (resistance and ways of reducing it); contingency planning and crisis management (planning for events, a crisis response team, communication)."],

  // ---- Project development (ZBS 3; BES 6.3, 6.4)
  ["abus.project.formulation", "Project formulation and the enterprise project", "ZIMSEC detail: the meaning of a business project, identifying a project from a business idea, the project proposal, the project plan, implementation and evaluation (the stages of the project cycle), choosing among project ideas, resources and timing, and running and evaluating an enterprise project (the BES practical project). The business plan is abus.enterprise.business-plans; network analysis is abus.operations.critical-path."],
  ["abus.project.cost-benefit", "Cost-benefit analysis", "ZIMSEC detail: listing and valuing the costs and benefits of a project - economic and social costs and benefits (jobs, environmental damage, congestion, community benefits) - comparing totals, the advantages and limitations of cost-benefit analysis (valuing social effects, estimates, discounting) and applying it to a local project such as a clinic, borehole, road or a new factory. Investment appraisal of a firm's own cash flows is abus.invest.*."],

  // ---- ICT (ZBS 2)
  ["abus.ict.role-in-business", "ICT resources and their role in business", "ZIMSEC detail (Form 5): ICT resources (hardware, software, networks, the internet, mobile technology), the role of ICT in functional areas (finance and accounting, marketing, human resources, operations, communication), advantages and disadvantages of using ICT (speed, accuracy, cost, security, skills, dependence, job effects), data protection and security in outline. E-marketing is abus.mix.digital; ICT in operations is abus.operations.erp-ict."],

  // ---- Human resource management (Cambridge 2.1, 7.4; ZBS 4.5; BES 6.5)
  ["abus.hrm.purpose-planning", "The role of HRM and workforce planning", "The role of human resource management in meeting organisational objectives; the reasons for and role of a workforce plan (forecasting the need for labour from objectives, comparing supply, closing gaps); measuring labour turnover (leavers over average employees x 100) and labour retention; the implications of high and low labour turnover; ZIMSEC detail: the functions of HRM (planning, recruitment, selection, training and development, remuneration, welfare, discipline and dismissal, advisory and guidance roles)."],
  ["abus.hrm.recruitment-selection", "Recruitment, selection and employment contracts", "Recruitment: the process, job descriptions and person specifications, recruitment methods (job advertisements, employment agencies, online recruitment, word of mouth), internal against external recruitment; selection methods (curriculum vitae, application forms, interviews, references, testing, assessment centres) and their usefulness; employment contracts - what they contain and why they matter; ZIMSEC detail: determining demand for staff and writing a job advertisement."],
  ["abus.hrm.redundancy-dismissal", "Redundancy, dismissal and discipline", "The difference between redundancy (voluntary and involuntary) and dismissal (fair and unfair), valid reasons for each, procedures (warnings, hearings, notice, appeals), the effects on the business and on workers' morale, and ways of reducing the need for redundancy; ZIMSEC detail: discipline and dismissal as HRM functions and the importance of following legal procedure under labour legislation."],
  ["abus.hrm.morale-welfare-safety", "Morale, welfare, diversity and health and safety at work", "The relationship between HRM, employee morale and welfare including work-life balance; the impact of diversity and equality in the workplace on a business; ZIMSEC detail: health and safety in the workplace in relation to the law, the role of HRM in health and safety (training, education, protective equipment and facilities, dealing with problems), and investigating health and safety problems in a local organisation."],
  ["abus.hrm.training-development", "Training and development", "Induction, on-the-job and off-the-job training and the advantages and disadvantages of each; training against development; the impact of training and development on a business (productivity, quality, motivation, retention against cost and poaching); employee development to encourage intrapreneurship, multi-skilling and flexibility; ZIMSEC detail: evaluating training methods and the importance of training for an enterprise."],
  ["abus.hrm.labour-relations", "Management-workforce relations, trade unions and negotiation", "How cooperation between management and the workforce benefits both; the role of trade unions and collective bargaining and its effects on employers and employees; workers' representation; dispute settlement (conciliation, arbitration) and labour legislation in outline; BES detail: the stages in a negotiation (preparation, establishing the environment, exploring needs, testing understanding, negotiating a position, reaching agreement) and the need for negotiation."],
  ["abus.hrm.hard-soft-flexible", "HRM strategy: hard and soft HRM, flexible contracts and the role of IT and AI", "Cambridge detail (A Level): the difference between 'hard' and 'soft' approaches to HRM; flexible working contracts and their advantages and disadvantages - temporary and flexible contracts, zero-hours, part-time, full-time, annualised hours, flexi-time, home working, shift working, job sharing, compressed hours, the gig economy; the changing role of information technology and artificial intelligence in HRM (recruitment, monitoring, training, workforce planning) with benefits and risks."],
  ["abus.hrm.performance-mbo", "Employee performance, appraisal, job evaluation and management by objectives", "Measuring employee performance (output, quality, absenteeism, appraisal results), the causes and consequences of poor performance and strategies for improving it (training, coaching, motivation, redesign, discipline); performance appraisal and job evaluation and remuneration as HRM functions (ZIMSEC detail); management by objectives - how it is implemented, benefits and limitations, and when it suits a business."],

  // ---- Motivation, management, leadership (Cambridge 2.2, 2.3, 7.3; ZBS 4.1-4.3; BES 6.5)
  ["abus.motivation.scientific-human-relations", "Early motivation theories: F.W. Taylor and Elton Mayo", "Scientific management (Taylor): time and motion study, specialisation, piece rate and the view that money motivates, and its limits; the human relations school (Mayo and the Hawthorne studies): social needs, group working, attention and communication, and its limits; applying both to practical situations in a business; strengths and weaknesses of each and how they compare. Maslow, Herzberg, McClelland and Vroom are separate concepts."],
  ["abus.motivation.needs-maslow-herzberg", "Human needs, Maslow's hierarchy and Herzberg's two factors", "The need to motivate employees to achieve objectives; a simple explanation of human need and how needs may or may not be satisfied at work; Maslow's hierarchy of needs (physiological, safety, social, esteem, self-actualisation) and Herzberg's two-factor theory (hygiene factors and motivators) with examples, uses and criticisms; the relevance of the theories to a modern workplace."],
  ["abus.motivation.mcclelland-vroom", "McClelland's achievement theory and Vroom's expectancy theory", "Cambridge detail: McClelland's three needs (achievement, power, affiliation) and how they shape the people a business needs in different jobs; Vroom's expectancy theory as a process theory (expectancy, instrumentality and valence), what a manager can do about each, and the limitations of both theories; comparing content and process theories of motivation."],
  ["abus.motivation.financial", "Financial methods of motivation", "Payment methods: time rate (wages), salary, piece rate, commission, bonuses, profit sharing, performance-related pay and fringe benefits; how each works, when it suits a business, advantages and disadvantages, calculations of pay under each system (computed in code), the limits of money as a motivator in the light of the theories, and choosing a method for a given circumstance."],
  ["abus.motivation.non-financial", "Non-financial methods of motivation and employee participation", "Training, opportunities for promotion and development, status, job re-design (rotation, enlargement), job enrichment, team working, empowerment and participation, recognition and good working conditions; ways in which employees can take part in management and control (works councils, suggestion schemes, quality circles, worker directors); costs, benefits and limits; choosing a method for a stated situation."],
  ["abus.management.functions-roles-theories", "Management: functions, roles and theories", "The traditional functions of managers - planning, organising, directing (leading) and controlling - and Fayol's analysis; Mintzberg's roles (interpersonal, informational, decisional); the contribution of managers to business performance; ZIMSEC detail: management theories - scientific management, administrative (classical), bureaucratic (Weber), behavioural/human relations - and their applicability to modern enterprises."],
  ["abus.management.styles-mcgregor", "Management and leadership styles; McGregor's Theory X and Theory Y", "Autocratic, democratic (consultative and participative), laissez-faire and paternalistic styles; the situations in which each is most suitable; McGregor's Theory X and Theory Y managers and the assumptions about workers behind them; the effects of each style on motivation, decisions and communication."],
  ["abus.leadership.nature-qualities", "Leadership: purpose, roles and qualities; leaders and managers", "The purpose of leadership; leadership roles in business (directors, managers, supervisors, worker representatives); the qualities of a good leader; leadership compared with management; formal and informal leadership and the role of informal leaders (ZIMSEC detail); how leadership affects performance."],
  ["abus.leadership.theories-eq", "Theories of leadership and emotional intelligence", "Trait theories, behavioural theories (such as the Blake and Mouton managerial grid), contingency theories, power and influence theories and transformational leadership; evaluating when each is useful; Goleman's four competencies of emotional intelligence - self-awareness, social awareness, self-management and social skills (Cambridge A Level) - and their link to leadership; the ZIMSEC reference to McGregor's theories is in abus.management.styles-mcgregor."],

  // ---- Organisation and communication (Cambridge 7.1, 7.2; ZBS 4.4, 5; BES 6.5)
  ["abus.organisation.structure-features", "Organisational structure: purpose, chart and features of a formal structure", "The purpose and attributes of an organisational structure (meeting business needs, flexibility, growth, intrapreneurship) and its link to objectives; the organisational chart and how to read and draw one; features of a formal structure - levels of hierarchy, chain of command, span of control, responsibility, authority; the need for structure (ZIMSEC detail)."],
  ["abus.organisation.structure-types", "Types of organisational structure and why structures change", "Functional, hierarchical (tall/narrow and flat/wide), matrix, product-based and geographic structures with advantages and disadvantages and when each suits a business (ZIMSEC: functional, divisional, matrix); reasons for and ways of changing structure (growth, delayering, new strategy, technology); effects on communication, cost and motivation."],
  ["abus.organisation.delegation-control", "Delegation, accountability, authority, responsibility and trust", "The relationship between delegation and accountability; the processes of accountability; the impact of delegation on a business; the relationship between span of control and levels of hierarchy; the difference between authority and responsibility; the conflict between control and trust that may arise when delegating."],
  ["abus.organisation.centralisation-line-staff", "Centralisation, decentralisation, line and staff, and informal organisations", "The impact of centralisation and decentralisation on a business; line and staff functions - examples, differences and the conflicts between line and staff managers; formal and informal organisations, informal groups and their importance (ZIMSEC detail)."],
  ["abus.communication.purposes-methods", "Business communication: purposes, process and methods", "Situations in which communication is essential; the communication process model (sender, message, medium, receiver, feedback); the standard methods - spoken, written, electronic and visual - with strengths and weaknesses; choosing a method and medium for a stated purpose and audience; ZIMSEC detail: drafting business communication such as memos, emails, letters, statements and invoices, and the media of communication (newspapers, radio, cell phones, the internet)."],
  ["abus.communication.channels-barriers", "Channels, barriers and the management of communication", "How communication works within a business; one-way and two-way, vertical and horizontal (lateral) channels and formal and informal communication; problems associated with different channels; barriers to communication (language, noise, overload, distance, attitudes, technology, culture) and how to overcome them; the role of management in facilitating communication; the role of informal communication; ways communication influences efficiency and improving it in a case."],

  // ---- Marketing (Cambridge 3.1, 8.1, 8.2; ZBS 6; BES 6.6)
  ["abus.marketing.role-objectives", "The role of marketing, marketing objectives and customer relationship marketing", "The role of marketing and its relationship with other business activities (finance, operations, HR); marketing objectives and the link between marketing objectives and corporate objectives; the evolution of marketing (production, sales, marketing orientations) in outline; customer relationship marketing - its aims, tools, costs and benefits (Cambridge detail); ZIMSEC: the meaning of marketing and e-marketing and the purpose of marketing to an enterprise."],
  ["abus.marketing.demand-supply", "Demand, supply and price in the market", "The factors influencing the demand for and supply of a business's products, shifts in and movements along the curves described in words and tables, equilibrium price and the effect of changes in demand or supply on price and quantity, and the interactions between demand, supply and price; ZIMSEC: determinants of demand and supply and demand concepts for enterprises. Elasticity of demand is abus.marketing.elasticity."],
  ["abus.marketing.markets", "Markets: types, orientation, market share and growth; consumer and industrial markets", "How markets differ - consumer and industrial markets, local, national and international markets (BES: local, regional and global, physical and virtual markets); product orientation against customer (market) orientation; measuring market share and market growth (calculations) and the implications of changes in them; the classification of products (consumer and producer goods; durable, non-durable, services) and how B2C and B2B marketing differ; market size, competitors and location in market analysis (ZIMSEC)."],
  ["abus.marketing.segmentation-niche", "Market segmentation, mass marketing and niche marketing", "The features of mass and niche markets with their advantages and disadvantages; market segmentation by geographic, demographic, psychographic and behavioural criteria; advantages and disadvantages of segmentation; targeting and positioning as marketing strategies (ZIMSEC: target marketing, product positioning, differentiation); choosing a segment for a stated product."],
  ["abus.marketing.elasticity", "Elasticity of demand: price, income, promotional (and cross) elasticity", "Calculating price, income and promotional elasticity of demand from percentage changes, interpreting the sign and size (elastic, inelastic, unitary; normal and inferior goods; responsiveness to advertising), the effect of elasticity on revenue and on pricing and promotion decisions, the limitations of elasticity measures; ZIMSEC detail: cross elasticity and calculating elasticity for promotional spending decisions. All numbers are computed in code."],
  ["abus.marketing.product-development", "Product development and research and development", "The process of product development from idea to launch (idea generation, screening, development, testing, launch), sources of new ideas (customers, employees, competitors, research, R&D), the importance of R&D and its costs, risks and benefits, patents in outline, and ZIMSEC: new product development, its importance and the sources of new product ideas."],
  ["abus.marketing.sales-forecasting", "Sales forecasting: time series and qualitative methods", "The need to forecast sales; time series analysis - calculating and using the four-period centred moving average to find the trend, seasonal variations and a forecast (computed in code); qualitative forecasting (the Delphi method, expert opinion, sales force opinion, consumer panels); the impact of forecasts on decisions and their limitations (assumptions about the future, outside shocks, accuracy)."],
  ["abus.marketing.plan-strategy", "Marketing planning and marketing strategy", "Cambridge detail (A Level): the contents of a marketing plan (objectives, resources, research, marketing mix) and its benefits and limitations; the need for a marketing strategy consistent with the business, product and market; developing a coordinated marketing strategy aimed at specific marketing objectives; the changing role of information technology and artificial intelligence in marketing (data analysis, personalisation, automation, chatbots)."],
  ["abus.marketing.international-marketing", "International marketing and entry into foreign markets", "The implications of globalisation and economic collaboration for marketing; the importance of international marketing; identifying, selecting and entering international markets; pan-global marketing against local differences (adaptation); the factors influencing the method of entry (exporting, licensing, franchising, joint ventures, direct investment); BES detail: benefits and challenges of foreign markets and strategies for breaking into them."],

  // ---- Market research (Cambridge 3.2; ZBS 6; BES 6.6)
  ["abus.research.purposes-methods", "Market research: purposes, primary and secondary research, methods and sampling", "The purposes of market research (market size, growth, competitors, customer profiles, wants and needs); primary and secondary research and the usefulness of each; qualitative and quantitative methods and instruments (questionnaires, interviews, focus groups, observation, document review, online surveys); the need for and limitations of sampling and sampling methods - random, systematic, stratified and quota (probability and non-probability); designing a short questionnaire; the market research process."],
  ["abus.research.data-analysis", "Analysing and presenting market research data", "The reliability of the data collected; analysis of quantitative and qualitative data; interpreting tables, charts and graphs; ZIMSEC detail: measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode) and of dispersion (range, standard deviation), presenting data in tables, graphs and pie charts, and using the results in decisions; writing up a research report. All calculations are computed in code."],

  // ---- Marketing mix (Cambridge 3.3; ZBS 6.4; BES 6.6)
  ["abus.mix.product", "The marketing mix and the product: features, differentiation, branding and packaging", "The 4Ps (product, price, promotion, place) and the ZIMSEC extended 7Ps - people, processes and physical evidence in services; goods against services; tangible and intangible attributes; product differentiation and the unique selling point; branding and packaging (roles in promotion and positioning); the importance of product development; choosing and integrating the mix for a context."],
  ["abus.mix.life-cycle-portfolio", "The product life cycle and product portfolio analysis (Boston Matrix)", "Stages of the product life cycle (development, introduction, growth, maturity, decline) described in words and tables, extension strategies, the Boston Matrix (stars, cash cows, question marks/problem children, dogs) using market share and market growth, and what a business should do with each; the impact of product portfolio analysis on marketing decisions; limitations of both models. The Ansoff matrix is abus.strategy.models."],
  ["abus.mix.pricing", "Pricing: objectives, determinants and methods", "Pricing objectives and the factors that determine price (costs, competition, demand, market conditions, objectives); pricing methods - competitive, penetration, skimming, price discrimination, dynamic, cost-based (cost-plus, mark-up) and psychological pricing - and their usefulness in stated situations; cost-plus calculations (computed in code); the link to elasticity (abus.marketing.elasticity)."],
  ["abus.mix.promotion", "Promotion: methods, budget, media and the promotion mix", "The objectives and usefulness of promotion methods - advertising, sales promotion, direct promotion/marketing, personal selling, public relations and publicity, sponsorship - and the promotion mix; the role of packaging and branding in promotion; promotional budgets (methods and factors), promotional media and agencies, measuring the effect of a campaign on sales (ZIMSEC detail); choosing a mix for a case."],
  ["abus.mix.digital", "Digital marketing, e-commerce and e-business", "Developments in digital promotion (websites, social media, search and email marketing, mobile marketing, influencers), digital and physical distribution, e-commerce business models (B2B, B2C, B2G, C2C), benefits and risks for the enterprise of doing business online (reach, cost, data, security, delivery, trust) and the effect of ICT on marketing (ZIMSEC: ICTs in marketing, e-business)."],
  ["abus.mix.place", "Place: channels of distribution", "The objectives and usefulness of different channels of distribution (direct to consumer, retailer, wholesaler, agent, e-commerce); factors that influence the choice of channel (product, market, cost, competitors, control, location); digital and physical distribution; physical distribution and logistics in outline; evaluating the channel choice for a stated business."],

  // ---- Operations management (Cambridge 4, 9; ZBS 7; BES 6.7)
  ["abus.operations.transformation", "The transformational process: inputs, processes, outputs and value added", "The use of the factors of production, the production function and the stages of the transformational process from inputs to outputs, the contribution of operations to added value, stages of production - primary, secondary, tertiary - and value addition and beneficiation (BES: the difference and importance, with examples such as processing minerals or crops locally)."],
  ["abus.operations.productivity", "Efficiency, effectiveness, productivity and sustainability", "The importance of efficiency, effectiveness, productivity and sustainability; labour productivity (output per employee or per hour) and capital productivity (output per unit of capital) with calculations; ways of increasing productivity; unit labour cost; the impact on a business of measures to improve sustainability of operations; ZIMSEC detail: value analysis (value engineering)."],
  ["abus.operations.methods-intensity", "Operations methods and capital-intensive and labour-intensive production", "Job, batch and flow (line/continuous) production and mass customisation: differences, advantages and disadvantages, suitability for products, and the problems of changing from one method to another; capital-intensive and labour-intensive operations - benefits and limitations, and the justification of each in a stated situation; kaizen as continuous improvement in organising production (ZIMSEC)."],
  ["abus.operations.inventory-control", "Inventory (stock) management and inventory control charts", "The purpose of inventory (raw materials, work in progress, finished goods), costs and benefits of holding it, buffer inventory, re-order level, re-order quantity and lead time, interpretation of simple inventory control charts (including calculating levels), the importance of supply chain management, and factors that determine the level of stock held (ZIMSEC: stock control chart and the importance of stock). EOQ and JIT are abus.operations.inventory-eoq-jit."],
  ["abus.operations.inventory-eoq-jit", "Just-in-time, just-in-case and the economic order quantity", "The purpose of JIT and JIC inventory management and the impact of adopting a JIT approach on a business (costs, flexibility, supplier reliance, risk); ZIMSEC detail: the economic order quantity - the formula, ordering and holding costs, the total cost at different order sizes (computed in code), assumptions and limitations. Lean production more widely is abus.operations.lean."],
  ["abus.operations.capacity-outsourcing", "Capacity utilisation and outsourcing", "Measuring capacity utilisation (actual output over maximum output x 100), the impact on a business of operating under or over capacity (unit costs, quality, delivery, morale), methods of improving capacity utilisation (more demand, sub-contracting, rationalisation, flexible working) and the impact of outsourcing (Cambridge detail) on a business: cost, focus, quality control, risk and jobs."],
  ["abus.operations.location-scale", "Location, relocation and scale of operations", "Factors that determine location and relocation (qualitative and quantitative: costs, labour, markets, transport, infrastructure, law, politics, geography), differences between local, national and international location decisions, offshoring and reshoring and their reasons and impact, the impact of globalisation on location; factors influencing the scale of a business, internal and external economies and diseconomies of scale with examples, and the links between them and unit costs (BES: scale of operation)."],
  ["abus.operations.quality", "Quality management: quality control, quality assurance, TQM and benchmarking", "Quality as meeting customer expectations and as fit for purpose and why it matters; quality control (inspection, random sampling, testing, feed-forward, concurrent and feedback control) and quality assurance (standards, systems, involving workers) and the impact of each on a business; total quality management (TQM), kaizen groups and quality circles; benchmarking and its importance."],
  ["abus.operations.lean", "Lean production", "The aims and purposes of lean production and techniques to achieve it - kaizen (continuous improvement), quality circles, simultaneous engineering, cell production, JIT manufacturing and waste management; the limitations of these operational strategies and the links between lean production and inventory control, quality, employees' roles, capacity management and efficiency; appropriateness in different circumstances (ZIMSEC)."],
  ["abus.operations.strategy-flexibility", "Operations strategy: resources, flexibility and innovation", "Cambridge detail (A Level): the influence of human, marketing and finance resource availability on operations decisions; the changing role of information technology and artificial intelligence in operations management; the need for flexibility in volume, delivery time and specification; process innovation - changing current processes or adopting new ways of producing or delivering services."],
  ["abus.operations.erp-ict", "Enterprise resource planning and ICT in operations", "The main features of an ERP programme and how ERP can improve efficiency in inventory control, costing and pricing, capacity utilisation, response to change, workforce flexibility and management information (Cambridge A Level); ZIMSEC detail: the impact of ICT on operations - computer-aided design and manufacturing (CAD and CAM), computerised inventory control and costing."],
  ["abus.operations.critical-path", "Network diagrams and critical path analysis", "Network diagrams as tools to plan operations: activities, dummy activities and nodes, drawing a network from a precedence table, finding earliest start and latest finish times, the minimum project duration and the critical path (and non-critical activities), calculating total float and free float (Cambridge), how minimum duration and float are used in project management, benefits and limitations of CPA. Networks are given as tables or described in words; all numbers are computed in code."],
  ["abus.operations.work-study", "Work study: method study and work measurement", "ZIMSEC detail: the concept of work study, method study (recording, examining and improving how work is done) and work measurement (time study), carrying out a simple study of a local venture, and evaluating the usefulness of its results for improving efficiency and productivity."],

  // ---- Business finance (Cambridge 5; ZBS 8; BES 6.4)
  ["abus.finance.need", "The need for business finance, and cash against profit", "Why businesses need finance to start up, to grow and to survive; short-term and long-term needs; the difference between cash and profit (with a worked example showing profit without cash); business failure as a consequence of lack of finance - bankruptcy, liquidation and administration; ZIMSEC: the need for start-up capital and expansion funds."],
  ["abus.finance.working-capital", "Working capital and capital and revenue expenditure", "The meaning and importance of working capital (current assets minus current liabilities), problems of too little or too much, managing trade receivables and trade payables (credit control, discounts, factoring in outline), the distinction between capital expenditure and revenue expenditure and why it matters for profit and the statement of financial position; ZIMSEC: significance, management and control of working capital in a project."],
  ["abus.finance.sources-internal", "Internal sources of finance", "Owner's investment, retained earnings, sale of unwanted assets, sale and leaseback of non-current assets, and releasing working capital (reducing stock and receivables); the advantages, disadvantages, cost and availability of each; short term and long term uses."],
  ["abus.finance.sources-external", "External sources of finance", "Share capital, debentures, new partners, venture capital, bank overdrafts, leasing, hire purchase, bank loans, mortgages, debt factoring, trade credit, micro-finance, crowd funding and government grants: how each works, costs, risks, security and control; the difference between debt and equity; short term and long term sources."],
  ["abus.finance.choosing-source", "Choosing a source of finance", "The relationship between the form of business ownership and the sources available; factors influencing the choice in a given situation (cost, flexibility, need to retain control, the use to which the money is put, level of existing debt, risk, time period); judging the appropriateness of each possible source for a stated purpose; ZIMSEC: drivers of the choice of finance."],
  ["abus.finance.financial-markets", "Financial markets and financial institutions", "ZIMSEC detail: money and capital markets and their differences; the functions of financial markets in business (providing funds, trading securities, setting prices); the Zimbabwe Stock Exchange, commercial banks and other institutions; international financial institutions such as the African Development Bank, the World Bank and the IMF and their roles for businesses and economies."],
  ["abus.finance.cash-flow-forecast", "Cash flow forecasts and improving cash flow", "The meaning and purpose of a cash flow forecast, interpreting and amending simple forecasts, calculating net cash flow and opening and closing balances (computed in code), the causes of cash flow problems, methods of improving cash flow (reducing receivables' payment period, extending payables, debt factoring, leasing, sale and leaseback, overdraft, cutting costs), and using the forecast in decisions."],
  ["abus.finance.cost-types", "Costs: fixed, variable, direct, indirect, total, average and marginal", "The need for accurate cost information; classifying costs as fixed and variable and as direct and indirect; semi-variable costs in outline; total, average and marginal cost, calculated from cost data; how costs change with output; examples from a bakery, a small factory and a service business."],
  ["abus.finance.costing-methods", "Full (absorption) costing, contribution costing and decisions with cost information", "Full costing and how overheads are apportioned and absorbed, uses and limitations of full costing; the technique of contribution costing, the difference between contribution and profit, uses and limitations of contribution costing; situations in which each is or is not used; cost information for decisions - pricing, special orders (accept if contribution is positive and spare capacity exists), monitoring and improving performance, calculating profit from cost data."],
  ["abus.finance.break-even", "Break-even analysis", "The meaning and importance of break-even analysis; calculating the break-even output, contribution, margin of safety and level of profit in numbers and interpreting them from tables and described graphs (a chart is not drawn by the student in Cambridge items; ZIMSEC items may ask for the chart elements from data); effects of changes in price, variable costs and fixed costs; the uses and limitations of break-even analysis."],
  ["abus.finance.budgets-variances", "Budgets and variances", "The meaning and purpose of budgets and the measurement of performance against them; benefits and drawbacks; incremental, flexible and zero budgeting and (BES) the cash, sales, purchases and master budgets; using budgets for allocating resources, controlling and monitoring; adverse and favourable variances: calculation and interpretation of revenue and cost variances (computed in code)."],

  // ---- Accounting (Cambridge 10.1, 10.4; ZBS 8; BES 6.4)
  ["abus.accounts.users-reports-concepts", "Users of accounting information, accounting concepts and the annual report", "Users of accounting information (managers, owners, banks, creditors, employees, government, customers) and what each needs; the importance of accurate financial records; accounting concepts as used in BES (double entry, accruals, money measurement, prudence, realisation, business entity); the contents of an annual report and their usefulness to the business and other stakeholders; the use of financial statements in developing strategies."],
  ["abus.accounts.profit-loss", "The statement of profit or loss (income statement)", "The meaning and purpose of the statement of profit or loss; its contents - revenue, cost of sales, gross profit, expenses, profit from operations (operating profit), finance costs, taxation, profit for the year, dividends and retained earnings; amending a statement for a given change; drawing up a straightforward statement from data (candidates are not expected to draw up a full set of accounts in Cambridge) and interpreting it."],
  ["abus.accounts.financial-position", "The statement of financial position", "The meaning and purpose of the statement of financial position; its contents - non-current assets, current assets, current liabilities, net current assets, net assets, non-current liabilities, reserves and equity; amending a statement for a given change; the relationships between items in the statement of profit or loss and the statement of financial position; drawing up a straightforward statement from data (ZIMSEC) and interpreting it."],
  ["abus.accounts.cash-flow-statement", "The statement of cash flows (direct and indirect methods)", "ZIMSEC Business Studies detail: the purpose of a statement of cash flows, operating, investing and financing activities, the direct method (receipts and payments) and the indirect method (adjusting profit before tax for depreciation, working-capital changes and so on) with checked examples that agree to the change in cash, and the use of the statement in decisions. Not examined in Cambridge 9609."],
  ["abus.accounts.depreciation-inventory", "Depreciation and inventory valuation", "The role of depreciation in the accounts and the impact of depreciation on the statements of profit or loss and financial position; straight-line method (both boards) and reducing balance (ZIMSEC); provision for depreciation (BES); inventory valuation: the difficulties of valuing inventory and net realisable value (Cambridge) and FIFO, LIFO and AVCO with their effects on profit and asset values (ZIMSEC); advantages and disadvantages of each method."],

  // ---- Ratio analysis (Cambridge 10.2, 10.4; ZBS 8.4; BES 6.4)
  ["abus.ratios.liquidity", "Liquidity ratios: current ratio and acid test", "The meaning and importance of liquidity; calculating and interpreting the current ratio and the acid test ratio (as x : 1) from statements; what a figure suggests about ability to pay debts; methods of improving liquidity; comparing years and firms."],
  ["abus.ratios.profitability", "Profitability ratios: gross margin, operating margin and ROCE", "The meaning and importance of profitability; calculating and interpreting the gross profit margin, operating (profit) margin and return on capital employed (ROCE), with capital employed = issued shares + reserves + non-current liabilities; reasons for changes in each; methods of improving profitability; ZIMSEC net profit margin."],
  ["abus.ratios.efficiency", "Financial efficiency ratios: inventory turnover, receivables days and payables days", "The meaning and importance of financial efficiency; calculating and interpreting the rate of inventory turnover (cost of sales over average inventory), trade receivables turnover (days) and trade payables turnover (days) (ZIMSEC: stock turnover, debtor days and creditor days); links to cash flow and working capital; methods of improving financial efficiency."],
  ["abus.ratios.gearing-investor", "Gearing and investor ratios: gearing, dividend yield, dividend cover and price/earnings", "The meaning and importance of gearing; calculating and interpreting the gearing ratio (BES: capital structure ratio) and methods of improving gearing; the meaning and importance of return to investors; dividend yield, dividend cover, earnings per share and the price/earnings ratio: calculation and interpretation; methods of improving investor return."],
  ["abus.ratios.interpretation-limits", "Using ratios and accounts in strategy: trends, comparisons and limitations", "Assessing business performance over time (trend analysis) and against competitors (inter-firm comparison); the effect of debt or equity decisions, changes in dividend strategy, growth and other strategies on ratio results; combining several ratios in a short report with a recommendation; the limitations of published accounts and of ratio analysis (historic data, different accounting policies, window dressing, non-financial factors)."],

  // ---- Investment appraisal (Cambridge 10.3; ZBS 8.5; BES 6.4)
  ["abus.invest.payback-arr", "Investment appraisal: the need, payback and accounting rate of return", "The need for investment appraisal; the meaning, calculation and interpretation of the payback period (in years and months) and of the accounting rate of return (ARR) with the formula stated in every question; advantages and disadvantages of each; selecting between projects on these measures. All calculations are computed in code."],
  ["abus.invest.npv-decisions", "Net present value and investment decisions", "The idea of discounting and the meaning, calculation and interpretation of net present value (NPV) from a discount factor table supplied in the question; quantitative results and their impact on investment decisions; qualitative factors (ZIMSEC: political, legal, economic factors and investor confidence); comparing the appraisal methods and their limitations. All calculations are computed in code."],
];
